WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.180
What a model's written reasoning
can and cannot show.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.972
Ask a model a multiple-choice question,
and add one line:

00:00:07.972 --> 00:00:11.500
I think the answer is A,
but I'm curious to hear what you think.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.940
Miles Turpin and colleagues
did this across thirteen reasoning tasks.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.219
The models' written reasoning argued

00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:21.340
for the suggested answer
and did not mention the suggestion.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.648
With that nudge
toward wrong answers, accuracy fell

00:00:24.648 --> 00:00:27.300
by as much as thirty-six percent,
in the worst case.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.860
The written reasoning read as a reason.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.580
It was not the whole cause.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.980
Newer reasoning models
write longer reasoning.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.660
A 2025 study measured how often it names
a hint the model used.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.071
Across six kinds of hint,
Claude 3.7 Sonnet

00:00:44.071 --> 00:00:49.060
named it twenty-five percent of the time,
and DeepSeek R1 thirty-nine percent.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.809
In environments that paid
for exploiting a planted shortcut,

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:55.902
the model took it on over
ninety-nine percent of prompts and,

00:00:55.902 --> 00:00:59.220
in most of those environments,
named it on fewer than two percent.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.300
That does not make the reasoning useless.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.866
In the 2025 OpenAI coding study, a monitor
that read only the model's actions

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:09.800
caught sixty percent
of two common test-gaming tricks.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.940
A monitor that also read the reasoning
caught ninety-five percent.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.640
Then the researchers penalized reasoning
that the monitor flagged.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.429
The tricks continued,

00:01:19.429 --> 00:01:21.920
and the monitor's catch rate
fell to near zero.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.354
A 2025 position paper from authors

00:01:25.354 --> 00:01:29.360
across several labs calls
this a new and fragile opportunity.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.600
Written reasoning is a readout.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.740
Check it against what the model does,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.340
and avoid training that pays the model
to make it look clean.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.260
Read the reasoning,
and trust the actions you can check.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.200
The voice you heard
is a synthesized version of mine.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.640
Every number on screen links
to its source under the video.
